# Does the computer in modern cars record the maximum RPM attained by the engine?

The short answer is yes, but the ability to retrieve that specific data point depends entirely on the architecture of the vehicle's network and the software tools available to you. In your 2013 Nissan Maxima, the Engine Control Module (ECM) absolutely logs real-time sensor data, including crankshaft speed (RPM), throttle position, and coolant temperature. Modern ECUs function similarly to embedded Linux systems or high-performance microcontrollers; they maintain circular buffers in non-volatile memory (often Flash EEPROM) specifically designed to store fault codes and historical events known as PIDs (Parameters IDs).

When you ask if the dealer can read this, you are actually asking about access layers. The disagreement between your local dealer and the one an hour away likely stems from their diagnostic capabilities, not a difference in car hardware. Your Maxima's ECM has been recording these metrics since the first mile driven. The system captures data at a rate often exceeding 10 times per second during normal operation. When the engine hits redline or experiences a knock event, the ECU flags this as an "event" and stores the peak values in memory until they are read or overwritten by new driving cycles.

To confirm this technically, you need to understand the OBD-II protocol. All cars sold in markets adhering to SAE J1979 standards must support specific data retrieval requests. The PID for engine speed is well-documented, but retrieving a *historical maximum* requires a different query than simply asking "what is the current RPM?". This is where the distinction between a basic OBD-II scanner and a professional scan tool comes into play.

A generic $20 Bluetooth dongle you can buy online usually only provides live streaming data. It tells you what is happening *now*. It cannot typically read the "Max RPM" history because that requires manufacturer-specific proprietary commands (Manufacturer IDs) that go beyond the standard open OBD-II definition. The dealer who says it's possible likely has a high-end tool, such as an Autel or Launch scanner, which includes Nissan-specific firmware allowing access to these extended PIDs. The local dealer saying "no" might simply be using a basic OBD reader that lacks the necessary decryption keys for that specific model year.

If you are looking to implement similar logging logic in your own software, the pattern is consistent across embedded systems. You maintain a circular buffer where every incoming sample updates a variable if the new value exceeds the current maximum. In code, this looks like a straightforward conditional check:

```c
volatile uint32_t max_rpm = 0;
uint32_t current_rpm;

void loop() {
    // Simulate reading from ADC or CAN bus
    current_rpm = read_engine_speed(); 

    if (current_rpm > max_rpm) {
        max_rpm = current_rpm;
    }

    // This check happens thousands of times per second
}
```

However, there is a critical pitfall in this approach: memory management. If your ECU has limited flash space, storing every single data point until the event clears can fill up the buffer quickly. Manufacturers often implement an "overwrite" strategy where the oldest events are discarded once the storage area is full. This means if you never read the data to clear the event flag, or if you drive hard enough to generate new events constantly, you might lose the specific instance of the highest RPM because it was overwritten by a slightly lower peak from a different drive cycle.

For your specific situation, the technical consensus is clear: the data exists in the ECM's non-volatile memory. The variance between dealers is purely a matter of tooling compatibility. If your local dealer insists they cannot do it without sending the car to Nissan for a proprietary software update or using a specialized subscription tool, that confirms the limitation is on their side, not the vehicle's hardware.

If you ever find yourself needing to analyze this data for tuning purposes, remember that raw RPM logs are often noisy. Engineers typically apply filtering algorithms or look at sustained thresholds rather than single-sample spikes caused by gear shifts or sensor jitter. For a deeper dive into how modern automotive networks handle these telemetry streams, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer relevant context on building robust data ingestion pipelines for IoT devices, though you will still need manufacturer-specific documentation to map the PIDs correctly for your Nissan Maxima.
